{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788138183,"nonce":"08H1fBef1Z3T/jczAbo7vBitV80=","title":"Wave 4 — learning. Depends: 4.1 (the playbook must exist first); the final","message":"escalation step additionally wants 5.1. Filed from a design conversation with\nEvan, session 5 (2026-08-30): \"lets file the checklist budget as a follow up\nissue.\"\n\n\nThe playbook must not accrete. 4.1 defines the escalation ladder (prose\nlesson → checklist line → template/policy); this spec adds the discipline\nthat keeps the ACTIVE surface small: a hard budget on the morning checklist,\nretirement evidence for every line, and a final escalation that moves proven\nlessons out of the agent's working memory entirely.\n\nRationale: the same discipline every observer got. The digest's byte budget\nmade \"an uncapped section is the defect\" a rule; the checklist needs its\nequivalent, or ten runs of post-mortems produce a morning routine no agent\nactually executes with attention. A checklist longer than the cap IS the\ndefect, not a large number of good ideas.\n\n\n- **Hard cap** on the morning checklist (N stated in the playbook itself, in\n  one place). Adding a line past the cap must force a recorded merge-or-retire\n  decision in the same commit — never a silent N+1.\n- **Escalation removes.** A lesson graduating into a template or policy\n  deletes its checklist line in the same commit (e.g. once the power-room\n  template carries a firefoam popper, \"check popper coverage\" leaves the\n  checklist for power rooms built from the template).\n- **Retirement evidence.** Each checklist line carries trigger metadata (what\n  journal signal or condition it watches, when it last actually fired). A\n  post-mortem pass flags lines whose trigger has not fired across N runs;\n  flagged lines are merged, demoted to a prose lesson, or retired — recorded,\n  not silent.\n- **Final escalation: checklist line → rwtest assertion** (needs 5.1). A\n  lesson whose check is mechanically expressible becomes a regression test\n  and leaves the agent's per-turn surface entirely. The playbook stays small\n  because its best content keeps graduating out of it.\n\n\nThe value of N (propose with rationale — what fits in one attentive read);\nmetadata format for trigger evidence (front-matter per lesson file vs a\nledger); whether retirement is per-colony or global (a desert lesson idles on\nan ice sheet without being wrong).\n\n\n- Demonstrated on REAL playbook content from actual runs, not hypotheticals:\n  at least one over-cap add forces a recorded merge/retire; at least one\n  graduation deletes its checklist line in the same commit; at least one line\n  is flagged by the retirement pass and dispositioned.\n- The cap and the ledger are readable by the play-loop (4.2) without parsing\n  prose — the morning checklist the agent executes is exactly the capped set.\n- A worked example of the final escalation exists once 5.1 lands (may close\n  with the first three bullets and file the rwtest half onto 5.1 if 5.1 is\n  not yet done — say so in those words rather than waiting).","files":null}]}